<h2>Key Takeaways</h2>
<ul>
<li>Cats are unusually sensitive to H5N1; Cornell says up to 70% of infected cats die.</li>
<li>Early signs are fever, low energy and not eating; breathing and neurological signs can follow fast.</li>
<li>Raw pet food (including freeze-dried raw), raw milk, wild birds and farms are the main sources.</li>
<li>There is no cat vaccine and no specific cure, so prevention matters most.</li>
<li>If you suspect infection, phone your vet before you visit.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What Is Bird Flu in Cats?</h2>
<p>Bird flu in cats is an infection with highly pathogenic avian influenza A(H5N1), clade 2.3.4.4b, a virus that normally circulates in wild birds and poultry. Cats catch it by eating or touching infected animals or contaminated food, and it often attacks the lungs and brain.</p>
<p>The same strain has spread to US dairy cattle, bringing it closer to farms and milk. The FDA notes that felines, from house cats to lynx and mountain lions, are particularly sensitive to it. USDA APHIS calls cats “typically dead-end hosts”, meaning an infected cat rarely passes the virus on to other animals.</p>

<h2>How Do Cats Catch Bird Flu?</h2>
<p>Cats usually catch bird flu by eating something infected: raw poultry-based pet food, raw milk from infected cows, or a sick or dead bird. Farms, and clothing or shoes worn around infected animals, are other recognised routes. The CDC says that, while rare, even indoor-only cats have been infected.</p>

<h3>Can cats get bird flu from eating birds or dead birds?</h3>
<p>Yes. The CDC lists contact with sick or dead birds as a risk for pets, and Cornell advises keeping cats away from them. Outdoor cats that hunt are therefore more exposed. If you find a dead bird, keep your cat away and do not pick it up with bare hands.</p>

<h3>Can cats get bird flu from bird poop or feathers?</h3>
<p>Possibly. Cornell explains that the virus spreads mainly through nasal secretions, saliva and faeces of infected animals, so droppings are a plausible source. The sources we checked give no specific data on feathers, but any contact with an infected bird carries risk.</p>

<h3>Can cats get bird flu from raw chicken?</h3>
<p>Yes. Raw poultry is one of the main links to H5N1 in cats, and several frozen raw chicken-based cat foods have been tied to sick cats in the US. The CDC’s food safety guidance says cooking poultry to 165°F (74°C) kills avian influenza viruses. If you cook for your cat, see our <a href="/tools/homemade-cat-food-guide/">homemade cat food guide</a>.</p>

<h3>Can cats get bird flu from shoes or humans?</h3>
<p>Shoes and clothing can carry the virus indoors, so Cornell advises changing clothes and footwear and washing hands after contact with livestock, poultry or wild birds. Person-to-cat spread is possible but unconfirmed: two Michigan indoor cats whose owners worked on dairy farms may have been infected by the people or their work clothes.</p>

<h2>What Are the Symptoms of Bird Flu in Cats?</h2>
<p>Symptoms usually begin with fever, lethargy and loss of appetite. Some cats then develop eye or nose discharge, laboured breathing, and neurological signs such as tremors, seizures, circling, poor coordination or blindness. The CDC notes that some cats die suddenly with few obvious signs beforehand.</p>

