<p>Sources: CDC, FDA and Cornell Feline Health Center. Many of these signs also occur with ordinary cat flu, explained in our <a href="/tools/cat-respiratory-infection-guide/">cat respiratory infection guide</a>. The <a href="/tools/cat-fever-checker/">cat fever checker</a> can help you describe symptoms, but only laboratory testing can confirm H5N1.</p>

<h3>What is the incubation period?</h3>
<p>The CDC, Cornell and the FDA do not give a firm incubation period for H5N1 in cats. Once signs appear, though, illness can move very fast: a 2025 systematic review in the journal <em>Animals</em> found a median of 2 days from first signs to death in cats where timing was recorded.</p>

<h2>Mortality Rate: How Serious Is H5N1 for Cats?</h2>
<p>H5N1 is frequently fatal in cats. The Cornell Feline Health Center states that up to 70% of infected cats die, and other reports show similar figures. Mild cases may be under-counted, because mainly the sickest cats get tested.</p>
