<h2>Treatment: What Can a Vet Do?</h2>
<p>There is no specific cure. Vets provide supportive care such as fluids, nutrition and nursing while keeping the cat isolated. The 2025 <em>Animals</em> review noted there is no standard veterinary antiviral protocol. Never give a cat human flu medicines at home; leave all treatment decisions to your vet.</p>

<h3>What to do if you suspect bird flu</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Phone your vet before visiting</strong> so the clinic can protect staff and other patients, as Cornell advises.</li>
<li><strong>Describe any exposure:</strong> raw food or treats, raw milk, hunting, or contact with farms. Keep food packaging so lot numbers can be checked.</li>
<li><strong>Separate the cat</strong> from other pets and avoid face-to-face contact.</li>
<li><strong>Wash your hands</strong> after touching the cat, its bowls or litter tray.</li>
<li><strong>Watch your own health</strong> and see a doctor if you develop fever or eye or breathing symptoms, as the CDC advises.</li>
</ol>
<p>A cat that stops eating can decline for many reasons; our <a href="/tools/cat-not-eating-guide/">cat not eating guide</a> explains when it is urgent.</p>

<h2>Raw Cat Food and Bird Flu: What Owners Should Know</h2>
<p>Raw pet food is the bird flu risk owners can control most directly. Several US raw cat foods have been linked to infected cats, and the CDC advises against feeding pets raw pet food or raw milk. According to the FDA, heat treatment such as cooking, pasteurising or canning inactivates H5N1.</p>
<p>Brands named in FDA and Cornell notices include Northwest Naturals, Wild Coast Raw, Savage Cat Food and RAWR; in September 2025 a cat was euthanised after eating frozen RAWR Raw Cat Food Chicken Eats. Since January 2025 the FDA has required covered pet food makers using uncooked poultry or cattle ingredients to plan for H5N1, a notice it reissued in September 2025.</p>

<h3>Does freezing or freeze-drying kill bird flu?</h3>
<p>None of the official sources we checked say freezing or freeze-drying makes raw food safe. The FDA and CDC name heat as the way to inactivate H5N1, several implicated products were sold frozen, and Cornell advises against freeze-dried raw foods and treats as well.</p>
<p>If you feed raw now, our <a href="/tools/cat-raw-diet-guide/">cat raw diet guide</a> sets out the trade-offs, and the <a href="/blog/cat-nutrition-guide/">complete cat nutrition guide</a> covers choosing a cooked or canned diet. Switch foods gradually.</p>

<h2>Can Dogs Get Bird Flu Too?</h2>
<p>Dogs can be infected with H5N1, but they are much less affected than cats. The FDA says dogs usually show mild signs and low mortality, and that HPAI had not been detected in US dogs as of its January 2025 update, although fatal cases have occurred elsewhere. The same precautions protect cats and dogs alike.</p>

<h2>Is Bird Flu Still a Risk for Cats in 2026?</h2>
<p>Yes. H5N1 is still circulating in 2026: USDA APHIS keeps updating its list of infected mammals and the CDC published new findings on cats and people in May 2026. The CDC still rates the risk to the general public as low.</p>
<p>That CDC report (MMWR) covered 19 sick cats in Los Angeles County fed raw milk, raw meat or raw pet food in late 2024 and early 2025. Of 25 exposed people whose blood was tested, one veterinary professional who had treated an infected cat without eye or breathing protection had H5N1 antibodies. The person had no symptoms and no onward spread was found; the CDC called this <em>possible</em> cat-to-human transmission. Handwashing keeps the risk low, and our <a href="/tools/zoonotic-diseases-guide/">zoonotic diseases guide</a> covers other infections shared between pets and people.</p>

<h2>Is There a Bird Flu Vaccine for Cats?</h2>
<p>No. The Cornell Feline Health Center states there is currently no H5N1 vaccine for cats, and the core feline vaccines do not protect against bird flu. Avoiding exposure is the only protection. Routine shots still matter for overall health; see our <a href="/blog/cat-vaccination-schedule/">cat vaccination schedule</a>.</p>

<h2>Prevention Checklist</h2>
<ol>
<li>Feed cooked, canned or heat-treated food; avoid raw meat, raw pet food, freeze-dried raw treats and raw milk.</li>
<li>Keep your cat indoors or supervise outdoor time so it cannot hunt birds.</li>
<li>Keep cats away from poultry, dairy cattle, farm buildings and bird feeders.</li>
<li>After working with animals or birds, change clothes and shoes and wash your hands.</li>
<li>Check FDA pet food notices and your brand’s recall announcements.</li>
<li>Call your vet promptly if your cat has a fever, stops eating or shows neurological signs.</li>
</ol>
<p>Indoor cats need outlets for hunting instincts; our <a href="/tools/cat-indoor-enrichment-guide/">indoor enrichment guide for cats</a> has play ideas.</p>

<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<h3>Can cats get bird flu?</h3>
<p>Yes. Cats are among the animals most severely affected by H5N1 avian influenza. Infections have been linked to raw pet food, raw milk, wild birds and infected farms. The illness often progresses quickly and many affected cats die, so safe feeding and limiting contact with birds are important.</p>

<h3>What are the first signs of H5N1 in a cat?</h3>
<p>The earliest signs are usually fever, tiredness and loss of appetite. These can quickly progress to eye and nose discharge, difficult breathing, and neurological signs such as tremors, seizures, stumbling or blindness. Call a vet promptly if a cat with possible exposure shows any of them.</p>

<h3>Can indoor cats get bird flu?</h3>
<p>It is rare, but the CDC confirms indoor-only cats have been infected. Likely routes are contaminated raw food or raw milk, and virus carried in on people’s clothing or shoes. Feeding heat-treated food and changing out of work clothes after farm contact removes most indoor risk.</p>

<h3>Can humans get bird flu from cats?</h3>
<p>It is rare, and the CDC rates the public risk as low. A May 2026 CDC report found H5N1 antibodies in one veterinary professional who treated an infected cat without protection, which it called possible cat-to-human spread. The person stayed well. Handwashing and keeping sick cats away from your face reduce risk.</p>

<h3>Is raw pet food safe during the bird flu outbreak?</h3>
<p>The CDC advises against feeding pets raw pet food or raw milk, and Cornell extends that advice to freeze-dried raw foods and treats. Several raw cat foods have been linked to H5N1 cases. Cooked, canned and other heat-treated foods avoid this risk because heat inactivates the virus.</p>

<h3>Is there a cure or vaccine for feline bird flu?</h3>
<p>There is no specific cure and, according to Cornell, no H5N1 vaccine for cats. Vets provide supportive care such as fluids and nursing while the cat is kept isolated. Because outcomes are often poor, preventing exposure through safe food and keeping cats away from birds is the best protection.</p>

<h3>Should I worry about my cat’s holiday leftovers?</h3>
<p>Raw or undercooked poultry scraps carry the same risk as raw pet food, so keep raw turkey and chicken away from cats while you cook. Fully cooked, plain, boneless meat in small amounts is a different matter; our guide to <a href="/blog/thanksgiving-foods-for-dogs-and-cats/">Thanksgiving foods for dogs and cats</a> explains which leftovers are safe.</p>

